### What happened?

*moving the discussion from https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/115918#issuecomment-1789808263*

One of our users has put together a synthetic test that seems to break the RepicaSet controller when using Kine, and I'm trying to track that down
1. Create 100 pods in a namepace, each with a unique label
2. In that same namespace, create 50 pods that each watch pods in that namespace, with a labelSelector that matches the unique label on one of the pods. Each of the 50 "observers" should see events from a single "observed" pod.

On 1.26 and prior, each of the 50 observers shared the cluster-wide etcd watch, with the apiserver providing filtering.

With this change, etcd logs show that each watch now kicks off a new pair of list+watch operations against etcd, which must then be filtered by the apiserver to apply the selectors. This is a significant increase in overhead against the datastore, and requires more work of the apiserver; I think operators should have been made aware of this change so that they could update capacity planning accordingly.

Here are interleaved logs from the apiserver (with --v=4) and etcd (with -log-level=debug --logger=zap) showing  1:1 correlation between apiserver watches and etcd range requests. Unfortunately it doesn't appear that I can get logs showing the watch that follows the range, but from tracing in kine I can confirm that this also occurs.

